===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|hight||to be named, be called}} (alternative past participle of {{m|enm|hoten}}), from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|hēht||tr=preterite of {{m|ang|hātan}}|to be named, be called}}, from *''hehait-'', reduplicate preterite base of {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*haitaną||to call, command, summon}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*key(w)-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*kyew-||to set in motion}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|heten}}, {{cog|de|heißen}}, {{cog|da|hedde}}, {{cog|fy|hite}}, {{cog|nl|heten}}, and {{cog|sv|heta}}, {{cog|la|cieō||I call, I set in motion}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|-|-|hight}}
''hight'' is only the [[preterite]] or past participle, not the infinitive or present.
# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To [[call]], [[name]].
#* Byron
#*: Childe Harold was he '''hight'''.
# {{lb|en|archaic|intransitive}} To be called or named.
#* Surrey
#*: Bright was her hue, and Geraldine she '''hight'''.
# {{lb|en|archaic|dialectal}} To [[command]]; to [[enjoin]].
#: {{ux|en|I '''hight''' ye take me wi' ye. I ne can no lenger her b'live.}}
#* '''1872''', {{w|John Stuart Blackie}}, ''[https://books.google.ru/books?id=9nACAAAAQAAJ&printsec=frontcover&dq=Lays+of+the+Highlands+and+Islands&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwjZvtHBhtPWAhVPFMAKHf4ACQMQ6AEIKDAA#v=snippet&q=%20I%20obey.&f=false Lays of the Highlands and Islands]'':
#*: Malaise priest of Innishmuurry / '''Hights ''' me go, and I obey.

=====Usage notes=====
The verb '''hight''' has many different forms in many different regions. For the present tense the form '''het''' is rather common. The usage example for the sense "to command or to enjoin" can be rendered in standard English in the following manner: 
* ''I '''hight''' ye take me wi' ye. I ne can no lenger her b'live'' = ''I '''bid''' you take me with you. I can no longer stay here''.
Moreover, the verb "'''hight'''", in the sense "to enjoin", is mainly used for emphasis, and as such is untranslatable into standard English. For example: ''I '''het''' ye leit mee men ga. 'Ey ne dyde nathing te na ane. 'Ey ar wyteless. (Please, let my men go.  They did not do anything to any one. They are blameless)''.

It also should be noted that the verb '''hight''' survives only as part of [[oral tradition]] in rural Scotland and Northern England. It is not used in common speech.

